# modules/network/checks-banner.rules - the NET-07 AND NET-11 script-check
# registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; data/scoursh-network-scan-design/
# report.md §3.2 item 1, §3.4, §5.1, the NET-07 and NET-11 rows in its §7
# staged plan). Owned by modules/network/banner.sh - see that file's own
# header for why NET-11 (the version->vulnerability lookup) lives inside
# NET-07's own phase script rather than as a peer file.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) - matching
# modules/network/checks-reachability.rules (NET-06), never a shared
# `checks.rules` two peers could collide on.
#
# TWO CHECK IDS, ONE PER PROPERTY: `NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01` (a banner
# disclosing a product name alone, and one disclosing a product AND version,
# are the SAME finding at two strengths of the same fact - net_banner_
# identify_text, modules/network/banner_engine.sh, reports both under this
# one id, with the version named in the evidence when one was disclosed) and
# `NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01` (an EXACT data/versions.db `banner`-
# namespace match on that same disclosure - report.md §3.4, never a range or
# heuristic comparison). The `net` location profile (lib/findings.sh,
# target/host/port/transport) carries no "kind" or "product" component, so a
# name-only disclosure and a known-vulnerable-version hit on the SAME
# listener would collide onto the SAME fingerprint under one id -
# modules/network/checks-httpport.rules' own header (NET-09) states the
# identical reasoning for its own two sibling ids, and it applies here
# unchanged.
#
# NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01 IS `confidence: medium`, NEVER `high` -
# report.md §3.4's backport problem: the version came from a raw TCP banner
# the listener volunteered unprompted, which is the SOFTWARE's own
# self-reported upstream version string, and a distribution that backports a
# security fix (Debian's or RHEL's own openssh packages are the report's
# worked example) does so under an UNCHANGED version string. An exact match
# against the vendored list can therefore name a host that is genuinely
# already patched, which is why `confidence` is capped at `medium` here and
# the finding's own `remediation` states the limitation in words - the
# identical caveat modules/network/checks-httpport.rules' own
# NET-SVC-HTTP_OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01 record carries for the HTTP-response
# channel.
#
# The type tag is `passive` for BOTH ids (report.md §5.1's own table for
# NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01, and modules/network/engine.sh's own
# phase-table floor, `banner.sh:passive`, which binds every check this phase
# script emits): the probe reads only what a listener volunteers unprompted
# on connect and sends zero bytes - the opposite of NET-06's own
# `safe-active` tag, whose header explains why a bare connect attempt earns
# that tag instead (a real connection to a port the operator did not
# necessarily expect a scanner to knock on). Sending nothing at all on that
# same connection is what earns both of this file's checks the weaker tag,
# including the outdated-component one - unlike NET-09's HTTP-channel
# sibling, which sends one GET and so is `safe-active` instead.
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is NET's cell (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's own
# NET row: the config/scope.conf target id), identical to every other NET-*
# check.

id: NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Network service discloses product/version unprompted
script: banner.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: A service greeting that names its own product and exact
  version lets an attacker select exploits for that product and version
  without probing for them first. Where the software supports it, suppress
  or generalise the banner (an SSH `Banner`/version-string override, an
  SMTP `smtpd_banner` set to a generic string, an FTP daemon login-message
  override) so it no longer names the product or the version. This is
  defence in depth, not a fix on its own: keep the component patched on its
  own schedule regardless of whether the banner is suppressed.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01
title: Network service version named in the vendored known-vulnerable list
script: banner.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Upgrade the component on this listener to a release the
  advisory does not name, or apply the vendor backport for it. Where an
  upgrade cannot be immediate, put a compensating control in front of the
  specific weakness the advisory describes and track the upgrade as the
  remediation rather than treating the control as one. Re-read the running
  version from the same listener afterwards. The version was read from a
  raw TCP banner the listener volunteered on connect, not a package manager
  or an authenticated check, so a distribution that backports a security
  fix under an unchanged upstream version string (e.g. Debian's or RHEL's
  own openssh packages) would not be visible here - confirm the running
  binary before treating this as a high-confidence claim about it.
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
